CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ function type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.type]/1, [dcl.fct]/5: two template-ids are the same when their
type arguments are the same type; decltype(cb) and void(int) are.  A
written function type template argument keeps its parameters in parsed
form (cpp_declaration, with the declarator part such as `&' or `*'
separate from the base type) while decltype(cb) is a converted code type
(parameters as `parameter').  The deduction of A from both arguments
conflicted, and the primary template was selected (bin53).  Partial
specialization matching and the consistency check of repeated deductions
now compare function types in converted form
(cpp_typecheck_resolvet::canonical_function_type).  The parsed form itself
is kept: the matcher deduces from it.  (decltype(cb) itself used to be a
pointer type; see cpp11_decltype_of_function_name.)
test_partial_specialization.desc, test_deduced.desc: tr<R(A)> and
tr<R(int &, A...)> for decltype, deduced and typedef function types.
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG
assertion.
